<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bobovac</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bobovac is a ruined royal fortress in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, near the town of Vareš. First recorded in the mid-14th century, it was a chief residence of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, several of whom were buried in its chapel. The Ottoman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> captured it in May 1463 after a short siege, a blow that led quickly to the fall of the Bosnian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bobovac.jpg" alt="Bobovac" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnToni · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bobovac_9_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bobovac stands near the village of Borovica and close to the royal court at Sutjeska, today the village of Kraljeva Sutjeska. Within its main walls the royal town held a large residential area, a group of places of worship with the burial chapel of the Bosnian kings and the Grand Church, and the royal court, set apart from the rest of the town by inner walls and forecourts and built with decorative elements in the Gothic style.</p><p>The court was fitted into five karst ridges on three levels sloping from north to south. It had two gates, two keeps, two forecourts and three palaces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a> was a building of at least four storeys, about 25 by 10 metres, whose north wall was formed by a stone cliff; its gate bore the coat of arms of King Tvrtko II. The second forecourt held a granary, a large water cistern, a smithy and a lime-pit. A room of the Upper Palace was probably the chapel of Tvrtko II's wife, Dorothy Garai, and its annex housed the workshops of the court's master craftsman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was built in the reign of Stephen II, Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and is first mentioned in a document of 1349. It shared the role of royal seat with the court at Sutjeska, but was much better fortified. The crown jewels of Bosnia were kept there. During his war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, King Stephen Tomašević moved the royal seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a>, and the fall of Bobovac in 1463 hastened the Ottoman conquest of Bosnia.</p><p>Nine skeletons have been found in the five tombs of the royal mausoleum. Those identified belong to Kings Dabiša, Ostoja, Ostojić, Tvrtko II and Thomas. One of the others is thought to be that of the last king, Tomašević, beheaded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> on the order of Mehmed the Conqueror, and the only female skeleton, beside that of Tvrtko II, is thought to be Queen Dorothy's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bobovac is protected as a National Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Commemorations of Statehood Day, 25 November, are held at the site, and on 22 October members of the country's armed and police forces make a pilgrimage there on the Prayer Day for the Homeland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bobovac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1463 Mehmed II invaded, captured <b>Bobovac</b> and executed the last king, Stephen Tomašević, at Jajce.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
